# EXPLAINABILITY

Version 1.0

---

# Purpose

The Explainability Engine ensures that every recommendation made by CAS can be understood by the athlete.

CAS never asks an athlete to trust a black box.

Every meaningful recommendation should be accompanied by an explanation that is:

clear,

honest,

evidence-informed,

and proportional to the athlete's needs.

Explainability is not an additional feature.

It is a fundamental property of the Combat Athlete System.

---

# Core Principle

Every decision must answer four questions.

1.

What decision was made?

2.

Why was this decision made?

3.

What information was used?

4.

What would have produced a different decision?

If CAS cannot answer these questions,

the recommendation should be considered incomplete.

---

# Philosophy

CAS is an expert system.

Not an oracle.

It does not claim certainty.

It explains reasoning.

The athlete should understand:

what CAS knows,

what CAS assumes,

what CAS does not know.

---

# Trust

Trust is earned through transparency.

CAS should never hide uncertainty.

When evidence is incomplete,

the explanation should acknowledge it.

Example:

Recovery appears lower than usual.

This recommendation is based primarily on your recent training load.

Sleep data was unavailable.

---

# Levels of Explanation

The athlete may choose how much information they want.

---

# Level 1

Minimal

One sentence.

Example:

Increase the load next session.

---

# Level 2

Standard

Decision plus short justification.

Example:

Increase the load.

You successfully completed the prescribed repetitions during the last three sessions.

---

# Level 3

Detailed

Decision

↓

Evidence

↓

Applied rules

↓

Alternative possibilities

↓

Confidence level

Example:

Decision

Increase Bench Press from 80 kg to 82.5 kg.

Evidence

Three consecutive successful sessions.

Average RPE:

7.6

No pain reported.

Recovery normal.

Applied Rule

Progressive overload rule.

Alternative

If average RPE had exceeded 9,

the load would have remained unchanged.

Confidence

High.

---

# Decision Structure

Every recommendation follows the same structure.

Observation

↓

Interpretation

↓

Decision

↓

Expected Outcome

↓

Confidence

---

# Observation

Raw data.

Examples:

Completed repetitions.

Training load.

Recovery score.

Sleep duration.

Heart rate.

Body mass.

Pain reports.

Subjective fatigue.

CAS distinguishes observations from conclusions.

---

# Interpretation

Interpretation transforms observations into knowledge.

Example:

Observation

Three successful sessions.

Interpretation

Current stimulus is well tolerated.

---

# Decision

Only after interpretation.

Example

Increase load by 2.5 kg.

---

# Expected Outcome

CAS explains what it expects.

Example

The new load should remain achievable while maintaining technical quality.

---

# Confidence

Every recommendation carries a confidence estimate.

Examples

Very High

High

Moderate

Low

Unknown

Confidence depends on data quality.

Not on optimism.

---

# Sources of Evidence

CAS should identify which information influenced a decision.

Possible sources:

Training history.

Recovery.

Performance.

Athlete profile.

Equipment.

Periodization.

Scientific knowledge.

Coach-defined constraints.

Unavailable data should also be reported.

---

# Scientific Foundation

Whenever appropriate,

CAS may reference the scientific principle involved.

Examples:

Progressive overload.

Specificity.

Fatigue management.

Recovery adaptation.

Motor learning.

The objective is education.

Not citation.

---

# Counterfactual Reasoning

Whenever possible,

CAS explains what would have changed the recommendation.

Example

Had your recovery score been lower,

the planned volume would have been reduced.

Counterfactual explanations improve understanding.

---

# Uncertainty

CAS distinguishes between:

Known.

Estimated.

Predicted.

Unknown.

These categories should never be mixed.

---

# Recommendation Types

Explainability applies to every recommendation.

Examples:

Exercise selection.

Load progression.

Volume.

Intensity.

Frequency.

Recovery.

Deload.

Exercise substitution.

Warm-up.

Combat conditioning.

---

# Safety Decisions

Safety recommendations require the highest level of explanation.

Example

Deadlifts have been replaced.

Reason:

Lower back pain was reported during the previous session.

Alternative exercises maintain the same movement pattern while reducing spinal loading.

---

# Recovery Decisions

Example

Today's conditioning volume has been reduced.

Reason

Recovery indicators suggest accumulated fatigue.

Expected benefit

Maintain adaptation while reducing injury risk.

---

# Exercise Selection

Example

Trap Bar Deadlift selected.

Reason

Matches the desired strength adaptation while reducing technical complexity.

---

# Session Generation

Example

Heavy Bag Intervals selected.

Reason

Today's objective focuses on anaerobic power specific to striking sports.

---

# Periodization

Example

Deload week introduced.

Reason

Training stress has accumulated over four consecutive weeks.

Expected benefit

Improve long-term progression.

---

# Learning

CAS should explain when recommendations change because it has learned something.

Example

Grip endurance appears to be improving faster than expected.

Future sessions will include a greater emphasis on maximal pulling strength.

---

# Human Override

The athlete may ignore a recommendation.

CAS records this.

Future explanations may include:

Recommendation declined by athlete.

No negative judgement should ever be expressed.

---

# Coaching Language

Explainability remains factual.

Professional.

Respectful.

Never manipulative.

Never authoritarian.

Never vague.

---

# Accessibility

Every explanation should remain understandable by a motivated athlete.

Avoid unnecessary jargon.

Technical concepts should be explained when introduced.

---

# Limits

CAS should explicitly acknowledge its limits.

Examples

Current recommendation is based on limited recovery data.

No sleep information was available.

Heart rate monitor disconnected.

Confidence is therefore reduced.

Transparency is always preferable to false certainty.

---

# Future Evolution

Future versions may include:

Interactive explanations.

Decision trees.

Scientific references.

Visual reasoning graphs.

Coach review mode.

Comparison of alternative decisions.

---

# Success Criteria

The athlete should be able to answer:

Why am I doing this exercise?

Why this load?

Why this volume?

Why today?

Why not something else?

If the athlete understands the reasoning,

Explainability has succeeded.

---

# Final Principle

CAS does not ask for blind trust.

CAS explains.

The athlete decides.